## 3. Verification: what "still functioning" can honestly mean

| Level | Check | Reliability | Possible in AI Studio app? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Structural | Zip opens; untouched files byte-identical to original (SHA-256) | Fully reliable | Yes |
| Static | JSON parses, JS/TS parses, imports resolve, no conflicting dependency entries | Reliable | Yes (limited) |
| Behavioural | Existing tests still pass | Only as good as the tests | **No** (needs a sandbox backend) |
| Slice smoke test | Target feature runs end to end | Possible, can be flaky | **No** (needs a sandbox backend) |

The UI badge must list exactly which levels passed and state "Builds and tests were not run."

## 4. Risk tiers and design rules

| Tier | Contents | Default |
|---|---|---|
| A (low) | Config and scaffolding: `.gitignore`, `.env.example`, CI file, README sections | Pre-ticked |
| B (medium) | Dependency adds or upgrades | Needs tick |
| C (high) | New or transplanted code touching existing logic | Explicit approval with reasoning shown |

**Design rules:**
1. Never edit the original; work on a copy and output a new zip.
2. Apply proposals one at a time; roll back only the failing one.
3. Treat every zip as hostile: reject `..`, absolute paths, backslashes, null bytes, symlinks (`0xA000` mode bits); cap size, file count and compression ratio; abort the whole job on any bad entry.
4. Check packages exist before proposing them (LLMs invent package names; typosquats are real).
5. Track licences; generate a `NOTICE.md` listing every added library.
6. Scan for secrets locally and redact before anything goes to an AI model.
7. Format only generated files. Reformatting existing files breaks the "untouched files identical" guarantee.
8. Set execute bits (`0o755 << 16`) when re-zipping, or scripts in `bin/` and `.husky/` silently break.
9. Prefer, in order: registry library, generated code fitted to the project, copied code. No copying from reference repos in v1.
10. Deterministic code first, AI second. Gemini output must be structured JSON validated against a schema.

---

## 5. AI Studio constraints and what this changes

An AI Studio app is a browser front end calling Gemini. No server, Docker or npm.

| Planned feature | In an AI Studio app |
|---|---|
| Safe unzip, hashing | Works (JSZip, Web Crypto) |
| Stack and intent detection | Works (rules in JS, Gemini for ambiguous cases) |
| Findings and proposals | Works (Gemini structured output grounded by registry) |
| Apply and re-zip | Works in memory, then download |
| Build/test verification | Not possible; structural and static only |
| Secret scan | Works locally (regex + entropy), before any AI call |
| Live registry verification | npm and GitHub calls mostly work; rate limits and CORS apply |

Later, a backend with a network-off sandbox (local Docker, optionally gVisor; install with `--ignore-scripts`, egress only via an allowlisted package proxy) adds behavioural and slice checks. E2B Cloud runs user zips on third-party servers, which conflicts with the no-secrets-leave rule.

Zip limits to start with (constants in one config file): 50 MB zipped, 200 MB uncompressed, 5,000 files, ratio under 100:1, nesting depth 20.

